original_problem:
Carol runs the user database of the music-streaming service TuneVault. The service has $U$ users, each with a unique username, and every user picked a password from a publicly known list of $L$ candidate passwords. $H$ is a secure cryptographic hash function; concatenation is denoted $\,\|\,$. The attacker Trudy can read and modify the database, and her goals are (1) to compute every user's password and (2) to change every user's password to \q{letmein} (which is NOT in the candidate list). Only hash computations count as work; comparisons and database operations are free. For each of the following storage schemes, describe both attacks and give the exact number of hash evaluations Trudy needs:
\begin{tightitem}
\item[(a)] store $(username,\; H(password))$;
\item[(b)] store $(username,\; H(password \,\|\, username))$;
\item[(c)] store $(username,\; H(H(password) \,\|\, username))$;
\item[(d)] store $(username,\; H(password \,\|\, H(username)))$.
\end{tightitem}
TuneVault also prototypes a Lamport-style one-time-password login: at registration the server sets $stored := H^{500}(pwd \,\|\, salt)$; at login $i$ the client sends $H^{500-i}(pwd \,\|\, salt)$, and the server checks $H(\text{received}) = stored$ and then updates $stored := \text{received}$. (i) Does the server need to know $pwd$ or $salt$? (ii) After a successful login $i$, what must the client send at login $i+1$, can it derive that value from the token it sent at login $i$, and what does it cost? (iii) At which login does an eavesdropper learn $pwd \,\|\, salt$? (iv) Trudy compromises the server and may overwrite $stored$; show how she logs in as Carol without knowing $pwd$, $salt$, or $i$.
